      <h2 class="title-section max-w-[18ch]">Upstairs, since 2021</h2>
      <div class="reading body-copy mt-6">
        <p>A lodge built this hall in 1911, for meetings, dances and long suppers. Its floor is the first one, maple, sanded four times. Maite Goikoetxea, who grew up around the corner from Grove Street and taught yoga in Seattle for twelve years, saw it empty in 2020 and asked Calvin Oduya, who was teaching Pilates in Portland, whether he'd ever wanted eight reformers in a dining room. They opened the next year.</p>
        <p>The building is on the city's geothermal loop, laid under downtown in 1983, which carries the hot water that has heated Boise's houses since the 1890s. An artesian well is one the ground fills by itself. That's the name, and it's why the floor is warm.</p>
      </div>
